  "finding": {
    "severity": "blocking",
    "description": "The accepted choice is uniquely longest in 17 of 18 items. Across the cohort, keys routinely state a complete two-part mapping and responsibility boundary while distractors are markedly shorter, often a single flawed action. The fact that an individual correct answer needs more detail can be legitimate, but the repeated cohort-wide form makes the longest-choice heuristic highly predictive without evaluating the mapping decision. This is the systematic longest-correct signal §4.3 says to remove; the 17/18 observation is evidence of the signal, not a numeric acceptance threshold.",
    "minimumCorrection": "Within the same 18-item unit, make credible competing storage/domain mapping alternatives comparably complete where the current length cue arises, or make supported accepted keys more concise without dropping a necessary decision. Do not pad, impose equal lengths, or remove meaningful choices merely to change a count. Rebind exact choices and their diagnostics; retain an option ID if its semantic answer remains the same, otherwise apply the existing option-identity rule."
  },
